1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,325
LabPulse is a small operations tracker for shared lab equipment.

2
00:00:05,275 --> 00:00:13,975
This walkthrough follows the working app, the delivery pipeline, the AWS deployment evidence, and the monitoring setup.

3
00:00:15,308 --> 00:00:21,358
The overview shows six instruments and the reports that affect their status.

4
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:27,745
A technician can open an incident, move a repair into progress, and resolve it.

5
00:00:27,745 --> 00:00:31,408
The equipment badge follows the active reports.

6
00:00:32,725 --> 00:00:36,562
The browser reaches an Nginx frontend.

7
00:00:36,512 --> 00:00:44,287
Requests under API go to FastAPI, which stores equipment and incidents in PostgreSQL.

8
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:50,937
The same backend exports Prometheus metrics, so the dashboard reflects real requests.

9
00:00:52,267 --> 00:01:00,642
The repository is split by responsibility: backend, frontend, Helm, Terraform, GitOps, and monitoring.

10
00:01:00,592 --> 00:01:04,817
The API tests sit beside the backend code.

11
00:01:04,817 --> 00:01:10,504
The deployment and dashboard configuration are versioned with the application.

12
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:14,987
The workflow defines the quality gate.

13
00:01:14,937 --> 00:01:20,912
The Helm chart supplies Deployments, Services, Ingress, and an autoscaler.

14
00:01:20,912 --> 00:01:26,000
Terraform defines the AWS network and EKS cluster.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,887
Each part can be reviewed without searching through one large file.

16
00:01:32,225 --> 00:01:35,487
Here is a real push to the main branch.

17
00:01:35,437 --> 00:01:43,650
GitHub Actions starts from that commit, so the pipeline result can be tied to a specific version of the repository.

18
00:01:45,475 --> 00:01:54,137
The backend job lints and tests the API, while the frontend job checks formatting and builds production assets.

19
00:01:54,087 --> 00:02:03,650
Only after both pass does the container job build, scan with Trivy, and publish SHA-tagged images to GitHub Container Registry.

20
00:02:04,975 --> 00:02:17,137
Terraform planned twenty-four AWS resources in Sydney: a two-zone VPC, public and private subnets, NAT routing, EKS, and a managed node group.

21
00:02:17,087 --> 00:02:21,312
The plan was reviewed before the short-lived deployment.

22
00:02:22,642 --> 00:02:30,342
In the AWS Console capture, the VPC resource map shows the two public and two private subnets.

23
00:02:30,292 --> 00:02:37,829
The private subnets hold the worker nodes, while routing and NAT provide controlled outbound access.

24
00:02:39,142 --> 00:02:47,404
The EKS cluster reached Active, the node group reached Active, and Kubernetes reported two Ready workers.

25
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:57,767
This Console capture is from the earlier live deployment of the same Terraform stack; the later CLI verification is preserved in the evidence log.

26
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:05,687
Argo CD watches the main branch and renders the Helm chart for the local Kind cluster.

27
00:03:05,637 --> 00:03:10,075
A committed replica change was reconciled automatically.

28
00:03:10,075 --> 00:03:16,725
The application showed Synced and Healthy, and the frontend reached three Ready replicas.

29
00:03:18,058 --> 00:03:28,845
The local cluster has healthy backend and frontend pods, PostgreSQL, three Services, an Nginx Ingress, and a backend autoscaler.

30
00:03:28,795 --> 00:03:36,170
The API summary also responds through the Ingress, confirming the route from browser to service.

31
00:03:37,475 --> 00:03:43,137
Prometheus discovers both backend pods, and both targets are Up.

32
00:03:43,087 --> 00:03:54,587
The API exposes request counts and latency at its metrics endpoint, which gives the monitoring stack application data rather than only container health.

33
00:03:55,933 --> 00:04:04,445
The Grafana dashboard shows requests handled, request rate by route, and ninety-fifth percentile response time.

34
00:04:04,395 --> 00:04:10,895
After traffic is sent through the app, those panels display data from Prometheus.

35
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:17,825
The AWS environment was kept only long enough to verify it.

36
00:04:17,775 --> 00:04:21,550
Terraform destroyed all twenty-four resources.

37
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:30,400
Separate AWS checks found no LabPulse EKS cluster, VPC, or reserved NAT address left behind.

38
00:04:31,725 --> 00:04:43,462
The result is a working app with tests, a guarded delivery pipeline, a demonstrated cloud deployment, GitOps reconciliation, and observable API traffic.

39
00:04:43,412 --> 00:04:49,275
The repository includes the exact setup steps and evidence for each part.
